      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1761 the French ceded Fort Michilimackinac in Mackinaw City to the British, who abandoned it in 1781 for Fort Mackinac on the island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mackinawcity.net/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The name Mackinaw comes from Ojibwe mishinii‑makinaang, meaning “at the place of many snapping turtles,” reflecting the region’s turtle‑shaped straits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackinaw_City%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mackinaw City sits at the southern end of the Mackinac Bridge, where Lake Huron and Lake Michigan meet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackinaw_City%2C_Michigan",
